<Positioned left="114" top="752"><Text fontSize="14" color="#5B6B7FFF" fontFamily="Inter" fontStyle="BOLD" letterSpacing="1">HOW THE FOUR REASONS WERE SPLIT</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="114" top="780" width="460"><Container alignment="TOP_LEFT"><Text fontSize="15" color="#33463BFF" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">The meter read 402 kWh this month against 271 kWh last month, and the month was one day longer. Heating is separated from everything else using degree days: 163 in September against 329 in October, at 15.5 °C as the base temperature. Everything that is not heating is charged at the same rate in both months, so the difference is volume.</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="600" top="780" width="446"><Container alignment="TOP_LEFT"><Text fontSize="15" color="#33463BFF" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">The remaining split is arithmetic, not opinion: the four bars add up to £34.66 against a bill difference of £34.66 (the 0.00 difference is rounding on the unit rate). Weather is the reason this bill moved, so the honest answer to "what can I do" is the list on the right, not a change of supplier.</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="114" top="920"><Text fontSize="13" color="#5B6B7FFF" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">Prices and meter readings are invented for this household; the degree-day method and the arithmetic are standard.</Text></Positioned>
</Stack>
</Container>
</Snapshot>
